#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125375} :activity/id #uuid "62796b32-9fb1-56cf-9089-9689399b7402" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048527 :topic/id #uuid "a715d0ff-c3bf-59f0-8f15-f8770c11ebac" :topic/math-academy-id 1892} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125375 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125773 :tutorial/content "A **hyperboloid of one sheet** centered at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ is a quadric surface whose equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(z-z_0)^2}{c^2} =1,\n$$\n\nwhere $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperboloid of one sheet\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=1\n$$\n\nis shown below. It has its center at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperboloid.\n\n![](images/0d/0d97e5ac26685b49bcdb3155866bd34ba83bd758f02035b500d3f594fc5716c8.png)\n\n   \n\nThe intercepts of this hyperboloid with the coordinate axes are as follows:\n\n- the $x$-intercepts are $(\\pm1,0,0)$\n\n- the $y$-intercepts are $(0,\\pm1,0)$\n\n- there are no $z$-intercepts" :tutorial/id #uuid "b15b84bd-ab8f-5ade-8289-c1795374ab94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3438 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9b1de06e-be8c-5534-9c53-59e61058ffb5" :step/math-academy-id 19457 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125379}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125376 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124503 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124312 :question/id #uuid "e85cfe0b-4ff6-5cce-9384-aeb930843b49" :question/math-academy-id "e-5265"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b079a5ff-e73a-592f-b16c-8740682538f3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050992}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488228}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain of a Hyperboloid"} :step/id #uuid "4ba1a392-70de-526a-ac89-008dd6fbf4e5" :step/math-academy-id 19458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125459}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125379 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125775 :tutorial/content "A **hyperboloid of two sheets** centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(z-z_0)^2}{c^2} =-1.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** This equation is similar to the general equation of a hyperboloid of one sheet. The only difference is the right-hand side is now $-1$ instead of $+1.$\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperboloid of two sheets \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=-1\n$$\n\nis represented below. It has its center at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperboloid.\n\n![](images/96/96ec4196c3e299d2e57cdb8a3a2a06094452bf05e2f66e43fc60c059077b305f.png)\n\nThe intercepts of this hyperboloid with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,\\pm1).$ The surface does not intersect the $x$- or $y$-axes.\n\nCalculating the intercepts of a hyperboloid with the coordinate axes is done in the same way as we've seen before with spheres and ellipsoids. Let's see some examples.\n\nThere is a simple way to distinguish between hyperboloids of one and two sheets by simply looking at the equations:\n\n- *one* minus sign indicates hyperboloids of *one* sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1\n$$\n\n- *two* minus signs indicate hyperboloids of *two* sheets:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-}1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d9a4fb57-50db-54d3-a354-5c56c44b5b3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3440 :tutorial/title "Hyperboloids of Two Sheets"} :step/id #uuid "4ed11bf9-ec94-5810-9687-5899a45bf9cc" :step/math-academy-id 19462 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125634}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124555 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124364 :question/id #uuid "1fbe37ed-9022-5c44-851c-0e66fe2fb79f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6078"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d28eda1a-a90b-57e9-969a-eb36552e413e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566207}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Hyperboloids"} :step/id #uuid "fb6a05b0-ef9d-5743-a9b2-a3a300f5e53f" :step/math-academy-id 22380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125459 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125804 :tutorial/content "So far, we've encountered hyperboloids whose axes of symmetry are parallel to the $z$-axis. \nLet's now consider the cases when a hyperboloid is oriented along the $x$- or the $y$-axes.\n\nFor a hyperboloid of one sheet centered at the origin, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- When the $y$ term is negative, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis.\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} + \\frac{z^2}{c^2} =1\n$$\n\n![](images/85/854a4e7506aa8e14fd19ceaadb5311a2d83596d27b33224272c41fa0639ea996.png)\n\n- When the $x$ term is negative, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis.\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} + \\frac{z^2}{c^2} =1\n$$\n\n![](images/ef/effc9d579d8d2ba00fec421fb1800639ccb4fba440e4518a5454d4ca282bc2d3.png)\n\nLikewise, for a hyperboloid of two sheets centered at the origin, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- When the $y$ term is negative, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis.\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} + \\frac{z^2}{c^2} =-1\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2b77432e38466d0983c196aed51eecd614096d971f21b0c68804e71a39548210.png)\n\n- When the $x$ term is negative, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis.\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} + \\frac{z^2}{c^2} =-1\n$$\n\n![](images/4d/4d3b7e21da4acb5c13320c248c9cbe220488f43cfcbe328ad0416b22f449d143.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "37ea72a1-3f48-5ba4-94e5-ad5a2e962723" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3850 :tutorial/title "Alternative Orientations of the Axis of Symmetry"} :step/id #uuid "2deb75ff-8eff-541a-bff6-4a30ea363312" :step/math-academy-id 22397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124650 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124213 :question/id #uuid "58966f53-5f83-5c86-8ffe-38ad0dffd2b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12555"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf44a846-8458-5e2f-bef3-72cf87a6035f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051028}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566269}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intercepts of a Hyperboloid"} :step/id #uuid "55a21c7e-3632-5bac-88a2-92a6bd3c409d" :step/math-academy-id 45274 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125877 :tutorial/content "The traces of a hyperboloid of one sheet are either ellipses or hyperbolas.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the hyperboloid of one sheet given by\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=1.\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle centered at the origin of radius $1$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/b3/b369958a609ba3861be5d32fbf3195b6feb241228f81e89bb116b29f80e38318.png)\n\n   \n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $xz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/4c/4c77b8758877ac88a73dece8c52f3b9cb650c4176edad5db020b138d70e0393b.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $yz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/0d/0ddcfc2e78166fa801a46deccd30c4cc2ddc12e81ae2e81508b5e13a374c208f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f3dfcfe4-330c-5142-8314-d09baa6e8acf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7836 :tutorial/title "Traces of Hyperboloids of One Sheet"} :step/id #uuid "f7656072-e089-540b-89c4-8df18945b7f8" :step/math-academy-id 45276 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125878 :tutorial/content "The traces of a hyperboloid of two sheets are either ellipses, hyperbolas, a single point, or the empty set.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the hyperboloid of two sheets given by\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=-1.\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is the empty set since the surface does not intersect the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/96/96ec4196c3e299d2e57cdb8a3a2a06094452bf05e2f66e43fc60c059077b305f.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $xz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/71/71d280f0b887b4805c882362dab8c9b13ef08b83f91904bce487ef749a67a6ab.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $yz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/34/3425d7f8df3a4dbbb5fa5763baf499e220215d0e33a0914859b8d1707074145d.png)\n\nThere are a few other cases we should note:\n\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=1$ is a single point (the vertex of the upper sheet):\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 - 1^2 = -1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,y,z) = (0,0,1)\n$$\n\nLikewise, the trace of our surface with the plane $z=-1$ is another point (the vertex of the lower sheet):\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 - (-1)^2 = -1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,y,z) = (0,0,-1)\n$$\n\n- The trace of our surface with the horizontal plane $z=k$ for $|k| > 1$ is a circle:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 - k^2 = -1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow x^2+y^2 = k^2-1\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0c91656e9656d4958de2b82f24ae05a6611d24d1cf0536384fbdd873d2e10e1d.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "16cf67e7-24e7-5523-991e-af37acd35e60" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7837 :tutorial/title "Traces of Hyperboloids of Two Sheets"} :step/id #uuid "3e256a50-c4bf-5ba0-8f3e-b65f84c0fe2c" :step/math-academy-id 45277 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125879 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the graphs a typical one-sheet and two-sheet hyperboloids centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/45/45359d1c73d9cdc721068dbee0e27bedb8cde622bae0615c16862e4157b2baf3.png)\n\n   \n\nNotice that a hyperboloid of two sheets is defined for any $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ In fact, for any point $(x,y)$ in the $xy$-plane, there are exactly two points on the hyperboloid. \n\nOn the other hand, the graph of a hyperboloid of one sheet does not contain points over the central elliptical region (close to the axis of symmetry).\n\nFor example, given the hyperboloid of one sheet $x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 1,$ let's find the domain of $z = z(x,y).$\n\nFrom the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & \\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the *exterior* and boundary of the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ for the hyperboloid $x^2+y^2-z^2 = 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\left \\{(x,y) \\: : \\: x^2 + y^2 \\geq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4d2a84b4-9a16-51d5-a343-bc7826406b8e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7838 :tutorial/title "The Domain of a Hyperboloid"} :step/id #uuid "203c24cd-0b43-5e99-91fc-7f9c44b7b75c" :step/math-academy-id 45281 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125376}}] :activity/title "Hyperboloids" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124695}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408098} :activity/id #uuid "88792370-e4b5-529c-9775-596955ef5e16" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048527 :topic/id #uuid "a715d0ff-c3bf-59f0-8f15-f8770c11ebac" :topic/math-academy-id 1892} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1367a913-bd30-5d46-b192-55918d608fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Two parallel lines: $y=2x\\pm 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336215} {:answer/id #uuid "21b8d9e0-4b1f-50b9-9504-2d80b0e4177d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An ellipse: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336346} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1cf904-9470-5d6d-bd73-91765fe6eab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Two intersecting lines: $y=\\pm 2(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336852} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef4b176-d408-5e50-bc9d-8a80f42c2948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337468} {:answer/id #uuid "ddad069b-ab04-5be6-931f-973051696d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336852} :answer-field/id #uuid "1567f6f6-2047-5c07-9a81-179bf8b695ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5e51ba70-807e-5821-b5e3-3514d515c2b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-71966" :question/problem "What is the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{z}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=1$ onto the $xy$-plane?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $z=1$, we substitute $z=1$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{1}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(x-2)^{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm 2(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two intersecting lines: $y=2(x-2)$ and $y=-2(x-2).$"} :step/id #uuid "1a9a8033-786e-5c6d-91e1-dc436b6bcbe6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408101}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61eca7e5-5fa6-5690-a884-1b0e9b6596f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321913} {:answer/id #uuid "6d07251a-4f5b-5fe1-a5c8-5c5e4e7f9bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}<0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565846} {:answer/id #uuid "8a23aa13-d540-5d99-be2b-3b822200ad45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565899} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7e4314-461a-51dd-8e7e-003b239d4df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\ge 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565900} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc62f3e-4d97-58eb-bb7f-08bb44e41d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\ge 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565928}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565900} :answer-field/id #uuid "787e8934-c73f-50c0-b941-8b2a7ad64fd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e7388c8d-bcb0-58d2-b80f-408abd20ed3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36388" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet  $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{3}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1 & =\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1) \\\\ (z+1) & =\\pm \\sqrt{3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1$, including its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5b45693d-0a41-5bcf-b5f3-32ea1b93b472" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408097}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd42f6f5-8e5b-53dc-b5eb-fcddd900ecf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408089} {:answer/id #uuid "53357543-e2c9-531c-90c9-df1aa8ac742f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\ge 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565809} {:answer/id #uuid "6e310c38-d0e1-5c20-a7e8-fcc4464b7b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565850} {:answer/id #uuid "e88bb104-5429-5d48-9b16-2281d5b1fabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}>1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566059} {:answer/id #uuid "f0fd2738-95bc-5ce8-af8a-007c2793d79e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408089} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e07a855-54d3-5672-a263-c28fae61891d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "28ec3dc9-c6d5-5b75-a09b-0f48f395ab8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164302" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $(x+1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=-1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9} & =-1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+1 & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9} & =(x+1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =9((x+1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+1\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "63434d20-1c50-54e2-b16f-b8c9f4afd976" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "551c53cb-cbf5-55c7-ba29-b85d13550b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282412} {:answer/id #uuid "7819d091-7c0e-5b7f-811d-3d6c71883b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$9$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282716} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad6b7a6-a742-5b64-8cb1-af1729ef0f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$18$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282886} {:answer/id #uuid "a9de43cc-4364-5236-b915-7e6ddcebb2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{6}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283162} {:answer/id #uuid "b5641acc-ae6d-5cbd-a8c8-1547afc76732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283264} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ba59a7f-90be-5278-8bf5-8ea5a019c2ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6cf5542-c57d-51eb-9c7d-5efe33d9469c" :question/math-academy-id "q-164380" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+(z-2)^{2}=-1$ and the plane $z=3$ onto the $xy$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $z=3,$ we substitute $z=3$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+(3-2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+1 & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $6$ and $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "6909febb-7229-5854-94b6-0907664d8d3d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408101 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408095 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e08b4887-48da-5258-b769-f2c5686179db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408090} {:answer/id #uuid "172e525a-825d-5e3f-b466-60fd613e7f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516158} {:answer/id #uuid "17558800-ef18-55b3-ad7e-9cffc30e4b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}\\ge 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516159} {:answer/id #uuid "530f0720-d51a-5a62-b99e-18a15d0fb434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516286} {:answer/id #uuid "82e3a295-901e-5dba-be99-3c91dbacf4cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516389}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516159} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4502bac-b95a-55f0-b8aa-e4531b3d53f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1fec2431-d065-5bba-8c18-02d476e1dc33" :question/math-academy-id "q-74014" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}-1 & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & =16(\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}-1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2} & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ which includes the boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e9134067-d19b-519c-be84-3c600bf7faae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408099}}] :activity/title "Hyperboloids" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408082}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488348} :activity/id #uuid "f044953d-ed45-518b-a59c-617113ac03a7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048527 :topic/id #uuid "a715d0ff-c3bf-59f0-8f15-f8770c11ebac" :topic/math-academy-id 1892} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28dfc208-68c2-5f88-8bd4-209d53beb5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282042} {:answer/id #uuid "36789465-716b-57b5-bcb5-c61d70e72a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487670} {:answer/id #uuid "389cbac5-a671-5c39-a922-51617d9c06fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}>1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487674} {:answer/id #uuid "639e7663-1b18-512b-aaa1-be4700f6151a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487764} {:answer/id #uuid "c59c6bc5-e7d5-524f-b87d-e788fbdad7bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}<3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487764} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac34746-de0d-5226-892a-e787c1d531e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cc95e142-b514-5431-9924-bdc1db9331dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-164307" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-(z-3)^{2}=1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-(z-3)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & =(z-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z-3)^{2} & =(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ z-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} \\\\ z & =3\\pm \\sqrt{(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2} & \\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the circle $(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which includes the boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "346ce6bc-5f2c-5170-a37c-8daa10ff6d75" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488342}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488333 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "051774ba-959a-5c1e-a9f4-ba2f7128c841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281733} {:answer/id #uuid "3dcc081f-8c87-52c8-b3e1-e3a8a332e58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487686} {:answer/id #uuid "a77b95d1-fceb-5a52-bb2a-611c7c686bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487882} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c48da9-56f6-56f4-b48e-a9ee4cfebf84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}>1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487969} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f233b9-83c6-5d23-a7ab-9cd273335e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}\\ge 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281733} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ed39c8-50b3-5c43-b5b6-cec23b5c61a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5e42866d-b20f-5a25-a416-745497c6d775" :question/math-academy-id "q-164305" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-{z}^{2}=-1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1 & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1 \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c766094c-6eab-58b2-b2f3-4c23eb53e41f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488340}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488340 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fcdf940-6360-5efc-815a-67685f2bcd39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487692} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2dd945-a79f-5959-bfa8-cdfafd1ac3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487834} {:answer/id #uuid "ad54fb20-d344-5be0-b621-c08138a7d222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}>1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487897} {:answer/id #uuid "c00ed966-6006-5b17-8212-cacd1e467d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\ge 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487928} {:answer/id #uuid "c13ff75f-ea2e-5e78-b3fd-6dfe315f448e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487692} :answer-field/id #uuid "751f8b2e-9f8a-59f7-8b55-6cf5ae31cd83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "756c31bd-d0b5-518e-8fa8-bf3e64b797d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-164306" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}-\\frac{(z+5)^{2}}{16}=-1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}-\\frac{(z+5)^{2}}{16} & =-1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1 & =\\frac{(z+5)^{2}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(z+5)^{2}}{16} & =(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1 \\\\ (z+5)^{2} & =16((x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1) \\\\ z+5 & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1} \\\\ z & =-5\\pm 4\\sqrt{(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "e921225c-624b-5c95-9668-3ca7e3b9bedb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488342 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58602c0f-accf-5f29-9ed8-3bc7fca41325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487746} {:answer/id #uuid "9f47998e-e870-56c4-baa4-8f352b659234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{z}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487862} {:answer/id #uuid "a290ce1e-061b-5f18-9739-ce332aa415a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{z}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487870} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8afb28-1c32-5f0f-a8c4-af9d56f35d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{z}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487959} {:answer/id #uuid "ecef13cd-61bd-588f-a9df-6b1c14e317f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488014}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487959} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb913cd1-fa9a-53a5-80ea-84008e65ffbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cfd858a6-a00f-5a94-8347-c41608b62519" :question/math-academy-id "q-164375" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the hyperboloid of two sheets $-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperboloid. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{0}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a hyperbola in $xz$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "ee6f7b73-925c-51b7-8db4-73cd3744c6e3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488348 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b38fc56c-0b29-550e-b109-d771f89562da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391953} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0c9da7-de70-5aea-8bf7-5c9b4d287b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391954} {:answer/id #uuid "003b79b6-3c82-5aed-b7a9-37fc9aef1484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391955} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa71829-34e0-581f-aa98-5fcdef3852dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Two parallel lines: $z=2x\\pm 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391956} {:answer/id #uuid "f376e4eb-51f9-5938-9cba-d55cb6cf1e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An ellipse: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391954} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d57934b-d5eb-5a5a-8448-b8cae29a9df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fcfedd1b-1d6b-51cf-8ffc-3860006da14a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36288" :question/problem "What is the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=-1$ and the plane $y=2$ onto the $xz$-plane?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $y=2$, we substitute $y=2$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+1-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-2 \\\\ -\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the hyperbola: $-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "fa19a58c-a70e-5db0-ade4-effabe26c294" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488333}}] :activity/title "Hyperboloids" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487565}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516976} :activity/id #uuid "95da094c-cb37-530d-9171-267dcdd5dd88" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048527 :topic/id #uuid "a715d0ff-c3bf-59f0-8f15-f8770c11ebac" :topic/math-academy-id 1892} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516876 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408095 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e08b4887-48da-5258-b769-f2c5686179db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408090} {:answer/id #uuid "172e525a-825d-5e3f-b466-60fd613e7f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516158} {:answer/id #uuid "17558800-ef18-55b3-ad7e-9cffc30e4b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}\\ge 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516159} {:answer/id #uuid "530f0720-d51a-5a62-b99e-18a15d0fb434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516286} {:answer/id #uuid "82e3a295-901e-5dba-be99-3c91dbacf4cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516389}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516159} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4502bac-b95a-55f0-b8aa-e4531b3d53f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1fec2431-d065-5bba-8c18-02d476e1dc33" :question/math-academy-id "q-74014" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}-1 & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & =16(\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}-1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2} & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ which includes the boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "045330c4-8b5b-55b5-b71b-fe9403aa1a72"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27163c60-302f-5873-ad00-05f9fc6cf2b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516192} {:answer/id #uuid "2e168002-1ed1-57b4-8e6b-f6204db8e27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}<0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516208} {:answer/id #uuid "844c12cc-6c29-5811-8962-30dc8d6a532a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}\\ge 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516393} {:answer/id #uuid "bea4c84f-b068-5e60-82b9-448a2bf9137c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516516} {:answer/id #uuid "f56fd478-739e-5168-a707-21a2be18cb60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516393} :answer-field/id #uuid "24b11784-26f9-538f-8ba3-52d50b1c6d87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7a7a1c0f-0f8c-51ee-845c-3e87e142c085" :question/math-academy-id "q-71859" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet  $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{5}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-1 & =\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{5} \\\\ (z-1)^{2} & =5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-1) \\\\ (z-1) & =\\pm \\sqrt{5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8} & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}=1$, including its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5a386b7d-1c57-5048-9724-e307c6b38500" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516954}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cdbf0ca-75db-5f90-8955-64242b699cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516139} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6b1552-a10c-5a67-a222-9a99ec23f185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516205} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2cfb5a-966e-5a34-9f6a-912523db2991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516430} {:answer/id #uuid "b228c86f-d810-5b29-9d46-f26a62956d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516481} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7489bd-6451-5b1e-97b4-a9223f471ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ and $2\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516430} :answer-field/id #uuid "0955de35-ea37-5ad6-adbf-c21830b3db59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30e8d5c3-bff2-59db-90d6-3878e212499e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71962" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{5(x-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{5(y-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(z-3)^{2}}{4}=-1$ and the plane $z=2$ onto the $xy$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $z=2$, we substitute $z=2$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5(x-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{5(y-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(2-3)^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{5(x-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{5(y-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{1}{4} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{5(x-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{5(y-2)^{2}}{16} & =-1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{5(x-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{5(y-2)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $1$ and $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "b7b019dc-5193-51bb-bdb4-64a14504da05" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1367a913-bd30-5d46-b192-55918d608fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Two parallel lines: $y=2x\\pm 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336215} {:answer/id #uuid "21b8d9e0-4b1f-50b9-9504-2d80b0e4177d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An ellipse: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336346} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1cf904-9470-5d6d-bd73-91765fe6eab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Two intersecting lines: $y=\\pm 2(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336852} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef4b176-d408-5e50-bc9d-8a80f42c2948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337468} {:answer/id #uuid "ddad069b-ab04-5be6-931f-973051696d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336852} :answer-field/id #uuid "1567f6f6-2047-5c07-9a81-179bf8b695ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5e51ba70-807e-5821-b5e3-3514d515c2b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-71966" :question/problem "What is the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{z}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=1$ onto the $xy$-plane?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $z=1$, we substitute $z=1$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{1}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(x-2)^{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm 2(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two intersecting lines: $y=2(x-2)$ and $y=-2(x-2).$"} :step/id #uuid "c7c29bd2-52a7-58da-bab6-7c5edcb62364" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516876}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b38fc56c-0b29-550e-b109-d771f89562da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391953} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0c9da7-de70-5aea-8bf7-5c9b4d287b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391954} {:answer/id #uuid "003b79b6-3c82-5aed-b7a9-37fc9aef1484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391955} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa71829-34e0-581f-aa98-5fcdef3852dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Two parallel lines: $z=2x\\pm 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391956} {:answer/id #uuid "f376e4eb-51f9-5938-9cba-d55cb6cf1e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An ellipse: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391954} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d57934b-d5eb-5a5a-8448-b8cae29a9df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fcfedd1b-1d6b-51cf-8ffc-3860006da14a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36288" :question/problem "What is the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=-1$ and the plane $y=2$ onto the $xz$-plane?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $y=2$, we substitute $y=2$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+1-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-2 \\\\ -\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the hyperbola: $-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "e804d623-c83b-5e42-aa9d-2d4da33cd8cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516908}}] :activity/title "Hyperboloids" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516104}]}
